---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

import SeeMoreInfo from '../components/SeeMoreInfo.astro';
const { services = "/services"} = Astro.props;

import mainImage04 from '../assets/images/background/04star-bottle-1.webp';



const Name = "Stellarwaves-5";
let TopColum2 = "WRITE YOUR MESSAGE OF LOVE, WE WILL SEND IT"
let TopColum3 = "HOW WILL YOUR MESSAGE OF LOVE BE SENT INTO SPACE?"
---
<Layout title={Name}>
    <SeeMoreInfo titleColum2 = {TopColum2}, titleColum3 = {TopColum3}>
        <p slot="column2">
            An “I love you” that was never spoken. A farewell that deserved to go further. 
            Share what you feel with the universe. Write it and send it from here, from this page. You write it and we send it into space. 
            <a class="blink-02" href={services}>To send your Message, just click here.</a>
            This section is dedicated to messages born of love: letters, promises, memories, sighs. In a universe where stars keep ancient secrets, 
            you can leave a mark that transcends time. Here, every word sent becomes light, traveling through 
            the cosmic void as a testament to what we feel. Because love has no borders, and now it has 
            no limits either. Your message of love is not just a letter; it is a space offering, a declaration 
            that transcends generations, languages, planets, and remains for all eternity. Because love deserves 
            to be heard, even in the silence of space. Every word sent is a light of hope, a promise that, no 
            matter where or when, reminds you that love remains our most universal language.
        </p>

        <p slot="column3">
            Your message of love will be launched into space with a beam of light using binary code—a <b>universal language</b> used by <b>NASA and most space agencies on our planet to send messages into space</b>. Although it is not a "language" in the human sense, its simplicity makes it a powerful tool for interstellar communication. All modern electronics, from computers to radio telescopes, are built on the foundation of the binary system. Spacecraft, satellites, and NASA ground stations are no exception.
            Not only messages, but also communication with spacecraft through NASA's Deep Space Network (DSN) are carried out via radio waves. These radio waves carry binary-encoded information, which is then decoded by computers on Earth. The process is similar to that of your mobile phone, and to the one we use to send messages into space, but on a much larger scale.
            In short, binary code is not only for sending messages, but is also used by NASA and all space agencies as an intrinsic language of digital technology that makes space exploration possible.

        </p>
    </SeeMoreInfo>
</Layout>
<style>
    body{
        background: center / 100% 100% no-repeat url(../assets/images/background/new/w49b_lg-1.webp);
    }
</style>


